      ":question/problem": "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right),\\qquad y=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nConsider the following table of values. Angles are in radians.\n\n| **$t$** | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $b$ | $a$ |\n| $y$ | $- \\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $c$ | $- \\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ |\n\nFind the exact value of $a-b-c$.\n\n{{result}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The $a$ entry is in the $x$-row under $t=0$. Substitute into $x=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a &=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(0\\right)}{4}\\right) \\\\ &=\\cos\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $b$ entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-1$. Substitute into $x=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b &=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(-1\\right)}{4}\\right) \\\\ &=\\cos\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $c$ entry is in the $y$-row under $t=-2$. Substitute into $y=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c &=\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(-2\\right)}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\tan\\left(- \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the three entries with the signs in the requested expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b-c &=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\sqrt{3} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $- \\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $- \\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ |",
